<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: PolyForm-Noncommercial-1.0.0

  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the macOS build, and DELIBERATELY holding
  one entitlement rather than the set a webview shell is usually handed.

  Apple's hardened runtime is required for notarization and switches off
  several things an app may need. This app needs one of them:

    device.audio-input
      The app is a voice recorder. The page records through `getUserMedia`,
      and under the hardened runtime a process without this entitlement is
      refused the microphone before the user is ever asked. It is the one
      thing the app does that a page needs the machine for, and it goes with
      `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` in `Info.plist`, which is the sentence
      the OS shows when the first recording asks.

  And none of the others:

    allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      An Electron app needs both, because V8 lives inside it and compiles
      JavaScript to machine code in the app's own address space. WKWebView does
      not: it runs the page in its own out-of-process content and JIT services,
      which carry Apple's entitlements rather than ours. Granting an app
      permission to write executable memory it never writes is a hole with no
      feature behind it.

    disable-library-validation
      Nothing unsigned is loaded — the binary is one executable and a directory
      of static files.

    device.camera / personal-information.location
      The app opens no camera and no location. The pairing sheet's QR scan is
      the phone app's; on the desk a code is pasted.

  Each entitlement the app carries has its argument written next to it, so
  the day another is needed the same is asked of it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
